<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qatar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as State of Qatar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qatar, officially the State of Qatar, is a country in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> that occupies the Qatar Peninsula on the northeastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>, with Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> as its only land neighbour. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-thani">House of Thani</a> has ruled it as a hereditary monarchy since Mohammed bin Thani signed an agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1868, which officially recognised its separate status. After Ottoman rule it became a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> on 3 November 1916, with foreign affairs and defence reserved to the United Kingdom. In 1968 Britain announced its withdrawal from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, and Qatar left talks on a federation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a>; it became independent on 3 September 1971.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qatar.jpg" alt="Qatar" width="330" height="130" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Qatar.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Moroccan resident of <b>Qatar</b> was sentenced in August 2023 to five years' imprisonment for criticising the King on Facebook, and Sahrawi activists are detained as prisoners of conscience.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Abroad it has kept 36,000 troops in Northern Cyprus since 1974, supported the coalition in the First Gulf War, served in ISAF, KFOR and Somalia, and holds bases in Albania, Iraq, <b>Qatar</b> and Somalia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Negotiations for a nine-member federation that would have included Bahrain and <b>Qatar</b> broke down over representation and the choice of capital, and both chose separate independence in 1971.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Obsidian travelled hundreds of kilometres: tools found at Dosariyah and at Wadi Debayan in <b>Qatar</b> came from south-eastern Turkey.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ubaid-culture">Ubaid culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Most surveys list Brunei, Eswatini, Oman, <b>Qatar</b> and Saudi Arabia as absolute monarchies.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
